  "notes": "COMMON PATTERNS:\nIchidan verb ({一段動詞|いちだんどうし}), intransitive. Takes を to mark what one is ashamed of: ～を{恥|は}じる.\n\nUSAGE:\nMore formal and reflective than {恥|は}ずかしい. Used when someone feels genuine shame about their own conduct or character, not simply situational embarrassment.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {自分|じぶん}を{恥|は}じる (to be ashamed of oneself)\n- {無知|むち}を{恥|は}じる (to be ashamed of one's ignorance)\n- {恥|は}じることなく (without shame; shamelessly)\n- {恥|は}じ{入|い}る (to be deeply ashamed)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {恥|は}ずかしい: embarrassing; embarrassed — covers both situational embarrassment and shame, much more common in daily conversation\n- {恥|は}じ{入|い}る: to be deeply ashamed — stronger, more literary form",
